#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# laplacian

#% text
# `laplacian(f, [x1, ..., xn])` gives the Laplacian of `f`, the sum of the second derivatives `d(d(f, xi), xi)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The variables are Cartesian coordinates.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
laplacian(x^2 + y^2, [x, y])
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
laplacian(x^2*y*z^3, [x, y, z])
#% output
#= 2*y*z^3 + 6*y*z*x^2

#% cell
laplacian(exp(x)*sin(y), [x, y])
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# `log(x^2 + y^2)` is harmonic in the plane away from the origin; `together` shows the zero:

#% cell
together(laplacian(log(x^2 + y^2), [x, y]))
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The divergence of the gradient:

#% cell
f = x^3*y^2; [laplacian(f, [x, y]), div(grad(f, [x, y]), [x, y])]
#% output
#= [2*x^3 + 6*x*y^2, 2*x^3 + 6*x*y^2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `grad`, `div`, `d`
